From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian McKenna <brian@brianmckenna.org>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 17:55:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(shader): read request memory in runs, and keep image
 table capture per record cheap

RequestMemoryView gave the SRT walk one word per call and searched its
regions for each, and offered no bulk reader, so an image table snapshot
read every record a word at a time. It now reads runs of words
(SrtRuntime::readWords, a copy per region) and tries the region, or the
gap between regions, of its last lookup before searching. A word that
straddles two regions, or lies outside them, still fails as before.

An address-based table column of 65,536 keys checked page accessibility
through a hash map for every record and appended a read range per
record, all sorted and merged at the end; consecutive records share a
page and touch the last range, so the last page's answer is kept and
touching ranges are joined as they are taken (the sort and merge give
the same ranges). Resolving the tables looked up the poison record for
every poisoned key; runs of keys with one code now reuse the last
lookup.

The results are the same: one title's 217 compute shaders, each compiled
20 to 40 times through the result memo with moving descriptor tables,
give identical SPIR-V, bindings and push data. Its bindless shaders,
whose 65,536-key tables are captured at every dispatch, went from 2.1,
3.5 and 6.9 ms a call (fixed tables, 64 table copies, 2,000 copies) to
1.0, 1.3 and 2.3 ms under Wine; other shaders were already at tens of
microseconds. tests/RequestMemoryViewTests.cpp checks runs against word
reads and coverage against the regions' bytes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
